Feature: The escape canary
  Network policy, the node firewall and RBAC are each checked offline against the render, and each can stop holding on the live cluster without a manifest changing. Two CronJobs in the namespace escape-canary, at the restricted level and denying traffic by default, keep attempting what isolation forbids, every hour on a minute of their own, three pods a run on three different workers: escape-canary-network, whose busybox resolves a name through the pod's resolver and opens TCP connections to the cloud's metadata address, its own node's kubelet and Talos API, the API server, the demo's hello and the secret store, and escape-canary-rights, whose kubectl, the Flux CLI image's, asks the API server for a Secret of flux-system and every pod with the one token of the namespace, its account bound to nothing in the tree. Each attempt's result is compared with the table expected.tsv, which gives every attempt its result while Cilium audits and once it enforces, with the reason; a check prints one line per attempt, naming it by its role and never by address, and exits zero only when every result is the table's for the mode the application is set to, which follows Cilium's audit setting in the render.

  Scenario: The escape canary is selected by overlay entries and builds nothing when it is not
    Given the lab-sto2 overlay, which selects the escape canary with two entries, the namespace directory escape-canary and the application's ks.yaml
    When it is built with both, with only the namespace directory's, with none, and without each application the canary names in dependsOn
    Then with both it applies the namespace escape-canary, its app-template-defaults ConfigMap, its default-deny NetworkPolicy and the Kustomization escape-canary, which deploys into escape-canary from its own app directory, waits, names cilium, prometheus-operator-crds, hello and openbao and substitutes ESCAPE_CANARY_MODE alone; the application builds the two CronJobs, the rights job's ServiceAccount, the two CiliumNetworkPolicies, the PrometheusRule and the ConfigMaps escape-canary-expected, holding expected.tsv, and escape-canary-scripts, holding network.sh and rights.sh, byte for byte, each under its own name without a hash and with Flux's substitution off, and nothing else; with only the namespace directory's, the namespace, the ConfigMap and the NetworkPolicy; with none, nothing of the namespace; and without cilium, prometheus-operator-crds, hello or openbao it is refused, naming escape-canary and the missing application

  Scenario: The canary's pods meet the restricted level with a read-only root, and only the rights job's pods hold a token
    Given the escape-canary application built as Flux applies it, its namespace as the lab-sto2 overlay builds it, and the admission rules of Kyverno and Gatekeeper as their applications build them
    When the namespace's labels, the exceptions list and the pods of the two CronJobs are read and evaluated
    Then the namespace enforces restricted, keeps its objects when it leaves the tree and has no entry in the exceptions list; each pod passes the restricted level by the evaluator pod-security-check runs, and both engines' rules, as user and group 65534, non-root, with the runtime's seccomp profile, no Service environment variables, no host namespace, host alias or DNS setting of its own, its container with a read-only root filesystem, no capability and no privilege escalation; the network pods mount no service account token and run under no account of their own, and the rights pods run as escape-canary-rights, whose ServiceAccount mounts no token for a pod that does not ask, and ask for its token; no volume of either projects a token; the network container requests 1m of CPU and 8Mi and is limited to 32Mi, as the drill's check, and the rights container requests 10m and 64Mi and is limited to 128Mi; and the network pods mount the scripts and the table read-only and nothing else, the rights pods also a memory-backed emptyDir of 16Mi as HOME, where kubectl keeps its cache

  Scenario: Each run of a check starts three pods that only three different workers can take, and ends within its deadline
    Given the escape-canary application built as Flux applies it, the table and the render of the tree, with its real charts
    When the two CronJobs, their job templates, their pods' labels and affinities, and every CronJob of the render are read
    Then escape-canary-network runs at minute 23 and escape-canary-rights at minute 10 of every hour, in the cluster's UTC, minutes no other CronJob of the render runs at; each forbids a second run while one runs, starts within 600 seconds or not at all and keeps one successful and three failed jobs; each run is an Indexed Job of three completions, three at once, no retry of a failed pod and an end after 300 seconds, which leaves a run room for every attempt of its check at its bound and ends it long before the next; the pods carry app.kubernetes.io/name escape-canary, their check as app.kubernetes.io/component and k8s-gitops/escape-canary "true", restart never and require a node without the control-plane label and no pod of the same Job, by their own labels and the Job's controller-uid as a match label key, on the same hostname, with no preferred term and no pod affinity

  Scenario: The canary's namespace denies traffic by default, its pods reach the resolver, the rights pods also the API server, and nothing else
    Given the render of the tree, with its real charts
    When the namespace's NetworkPolicy, the canary's two CiliumNetworkPolicies and the flows of its pods are read and evaluated
    Then the namespace holds the default deny and is not listed, and its policies are that and the two; escape-canary-resolver lets every pod labelled k8s-gitops/escape-canary reach the cluster's resolver on 53, UDP and TCP, and escape-canary-rights-api-server lets the rights pods reach the API server on 443 and 6443, with no ingress rule in either; the network pods reach the resolver and not its other ports, the API server, the world, their node or another node, hello, the secret store, the proxies, Prometheus or another application; the rights pods reach the resolver and the API server and none of the others; a pod of the namespace without the canary's labels reaches nothing; and nothing reaches a pod of the canary, not the host, another node, the world, hello, the proxies or the other check's pods

  Scenario: The table gives every attempt a result for each mode, and its enforcement column is what the policies of the render allow
    Given the table expected.tsv, the render of the tree, with its real charts, the cluster's DNS domain from its machine configuration and the node firewall's listeners
    When each row is read and its reach is evaluated from the CronJob of its check
    Then the table says what each column holds, and its rows are, in order, the network attempts resolver, metadata, kubelet, talos-api, api-server, hello and secret-store and the rights attempts read-secret and list-pods, each with eight fields, a target and port of its kind, a result of the six for each mode and a reason; the resolver row asks the API server's name of the cluster's domain on 53/udp of the resolver's pods, the metadata row the link-local metadata address on 80 as the world, the node rows their own node as the host, the API server row its name on 443 as the API server on the port of its listener, and the hello and secret-store rows the Services hello in demo and openbao in openbao by their names of the cluster's domain on the ports the render gives them, as the pods the Services select on the container ports those ports target; every reach is answered, and denied exactly where the enforcement column says blocked; and the rendered cilium-config answers a packet a policy denies with nothing, policy-deny-response none, so a denied attempt is blocked

  Scenario: The table's audit column for the pod's own node follows the node firewall, and nothing else is blocked while Cilium audits
    Given the table expected.tsv, the node firewall's listeners in talos/firewall.yaml and the ingress default action of talos/patches/all/firewall.yaml
    When each row whose target is the pod's own node is held to the listener on its port and every other row's audit column is read
    Then the node's default action blocks; the kubelet row is open while Cilium audits, as its listener admits pods, and the talos-api row blocked, as its listener does not; and no other row is blocked while Cilium audits, as the agents drop nothing a policy denies at layers 3 and 4

  Scenario: The canary's mode follows Cilium's audit setting in the render
    Given the escape-canary application built as Flux applies it, its ks.yaml, the render of the Cilium chart and the docs
    When ESCAPE_CANARY_MODE, the CronJobs' CANARY_MODE and the rendered cilium-config are read
    Then ks.yaml substitutes audit exactly when the rendered cilium-config holds policy-audit-mode "true", and enforce exactly when it does not, the failure saying which to set; both CronJobs take CANARY_MODE from that variable, which the build substitutes; and the docs' step that makes the cluster enforce sets it to enforce in the same pull request, and the step back reverses it

  Scenario: A check exits zero only when every result is the table's for its mode, prints one line for each attempt and names no address
    Given the escape-canary application built as Flux applies it, and network.sh and rights.sh as its CronJobs' containers run them, by their command, from the ConfigMaps they mount and with their variables, on the host's sh, with stand-ins for nc, nslookup, timeout and kubectl on PATH that print busybox's and kubectl's messages, addresses included, and record their arguments, and, where a docker daemon answers, the pinned busybox and Flux CLI images with no network, a read-only root, user 65534 and no capability
    When each check runs every row of each mode as the table gives it, then with each result changed in turn, with an unknown mode, with no table, and network.sh runs in busybox against a listener, a closed port, a listener whose queue is full and a resolver that answers one name, never answers or is not there, on its loopback, and rights.sh in the Flux CLI image with a kubectl that never answers
    Then each container runs /bin/sh with its check's own script from escape-canary-scripts, reads the table its CANARY_TABLE names in escape-canary-expected and takes the node's name from the pod's spec.nodeName and, in the network check, the node's address from its status.hostIP; each check makes every attempt of its own rows in the table's order, a TCP attempt as nc -z -v -v -w 3 to the name, the metadata address or its node's address, the resolver attempt as nslookup under timeout 5, and the rights attempts as kubectl get of the Secret cluster-secrets in flux-system and of every pod, by name, under timeout -s KILL 10 and with no flag that turns away from the pod's in-cluster configuration; it prints one line for each, escape-canary, the check, the mode, the node, the attempt, the result, the expected result and ok or UNEXPECTED, then a summary line, and exits 0 when every result is the table's, 1 when one is not, naming it, and 2 with the reason for an unknown mode or a table it cannot read; no line holds an IPv4 literal or the metadata address, and nothing the tools printed; and in busybox its own sh, nc, nslookup and timeout give open for the listener by its address, the node's and its name, refused for the closed port, blocked for the full listener and the silent resolver, unresolved for a name the resolver does not know and refused for no resolver, and in the Flux CLI image its sh and timeout give blocked for the kubectl that never answers, each check printing its lines and nothing else

  Scenario: The rights job's account is bound to nothing in the tree
    Given the escape-canary application built as Flux applies it and the render of the tree, with its real charts, every application's build and every chart's objects
    When every RoleBinding and ClusterRoleBinding of the render is read
    Then the application builds no role or binding, and no binding of the render names the account escape-canary-rights, its user name, the group of the namespace's accounts, every account or every authenticated caller

  Scenario: The canary's images are pinned by tag and digest, and its kubectl is the Flux CLI of the repository's pin
    Given the escape-canary application built as Flux applies it, the drill's check, OpenBao's values, mise.toml and Renovate's record of what it extracts and its groups
    When the images of the two CronJobs are read
    Then the network container runs docker.io/library/busybox and the rights container ghcr.io/fluxcd/flux-cli, each by a release tag and a sha256 digest; the busybox is the drill's check's, by the same reference, and the Flux CLI OpenBao's guard's, whose tag is v and the flux2 pin of mise.toml; Renovate's kubernetes manager reads both from the CronJobs' manifests, with no comment of its own, and the Flux group moves the Flux CLI image with the flux2 pin

  Scenario: Falco keeps the canary's attempts in its record
    Given Falco's local rules in its values, the network check run with stand-ins, and the docs
    When every item of the local rules and the command lines of the canary's nc are read
    Then no item names escape-canary, and every item but the one for what Falco places in no container is held to namespaces among which escape-canary is not, so no item excepts the canary; no nc command line holds the -e or -c that the image's netcat rule matches; and the docs' tuning section says which rule the canary's attempts raise, at what priority, how often and how to find them
